# Socio-Religious Reform Movements of the 19th Century

> Colonial rule, English education, and the printing press forced
> nineteenth-century India to examine its own society. From
> Rammohan Roy's Brahmo Samaj to Vivekananda's Ramakrishna Mission
> and Sir Syed's Aligarh college, reformers fought sati, caste,
> child marriage, and dogma — and in doing so invented the modern
> Indian public sphere.

## 1. Why Reform Became Urgent

British conquest and modern institutions — courts, censuses,
missionary schools, and after Macaulay's Minute (1835) English
education — threw Indian society into self-scrutiny. The printing
press multiplied debate: Rammohan Roy's Bengali *Sambad Kaumudi*
(1821) and Persian *Mirat-ul-Akhbar* (1822) were among the first
journals to argue policy with the public. The reformers' targets
were concrete: sati, female infanticide, child marriage, enforced
widowhood, polygamy, untouchability, and the caste system's
exclusions.

[info title="The scale of the evil"]
Bengal's officials recorded more than 8,000 cases of sati between
1815 and 1828 — the great majority of women burned within a few
miles of Calcutta, the empire's most modern city. The reformers'
first battles were fought with exactly such figures, gathered by
the new tools of survey and statistics.
[/info]

## 2. Rammohan Roy: Father of Modern India

**Raja Rammohan Roy** (1772-1833), a scholar of Sanskrit, Persian,
Arabic, and English from a Bengal Brahman family, argued
monotheism from the Upanishads in his *Tuhfat-ul-Muwahhidin* and
*Vedanta* texts, founded the **Atmiya Sabha** (1814), and in
August 1828 the **Brahmo Sabha** — soon the Brahmo Samaj — to
worship one formless God without idols, caste, or superstitious
rites.

| Field | Rammohan Roy's work |
| --- | --- |
| Against sati | Petitions, tracts, and shastric arguments that helped bring Regulation XVII (1829) |
| Religion | Precepts of Jesus (1820); Vedanta-based monotheism |
| Press | Sambad Kaumudi (Bengali), Mirat-ul-Akhbar (Persian) |
| Education | Helped found Hindu College (1817); Vedanta College |
| Civil rights | Fought press censorship; demanded jury trial for Indians |

His campaign, supported by Dwarakanath Tagore and opposed by the
Dharma Sabha, bore fruit when Governor-General **William
Bentinck** declared sati illegal in 1829. Sent to England as the
Mughal emperor's envoy, Roy died at Bristol in 1833 — hailed ever
after as the herald of a new age.

## 3. The Brahmo Samaj after Roy

**Debendranath Tagore** (1817-1905) reorganised the Samaj,
founding the **Tattvabodhini Sabha** (1839) and its Patrika. His
brilliant recruit **Keshab Chandra Sen** pushed social reform —
intercaste marriage, widow remarriage — too far for the orthodox
wing: the Samaj split in 1866 (Brahmo Samaj of India) and again in
1878, when Keshab's daughter's child marriage to a Cooch Behar
prince scandalised his own followers and produced the **Sadharan
Brahmo Samaj**. The Brahmo Marriage Act (1872) legalised their
reformed weddings.

## 4. Vidyasagar and the Campaign for Women

**Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar** (1820-1891), principal of Sanskrit
College, wrote the primer *Borno Porichoy*, ran Bethune School
(founded 1849), and marshalled classical citations and about a
thousand signatures for the **Hindu Widows' Remarriage Act,
1856** — the first great legislative reform won by Indian
agitation. Bengali reformers fought polygamy and child marriage in
the press; the first legalised remarriage followed within a year.

Later in the century the **Age of Consent Act (1891)** raised the
age of intercourse within marriage from ten to twelve, after a
fierce public campaign — evidence that women's questions had
become national ones. Pioneer women's institutions — Bethune
College, Pandita Ramabai's Sharada Sadan (1889) — carried educated
women into the movement as its own leaders.

[definition]
**Widow remarriage agitation** — the first reform campaign to
combine shastric scholarship, signatures, lobbying, and
legislation. Its success (1856) set the template for a century of
reform politics: public opinion, not royal decree, as the engine
of social change.
[/definition]

## 5. Dayananda Saraswati and the Arya Samaj

**Swami Dayananda Saraswati** (1824-1883), a Gujarati sannyasi
trained under Swami Virjanand, founded the **Arya Samaj in Bombay
in 1875**. His battle-cry — **"Back to the Vedas"** — declared the
Vedas infallible and everything since (idolatry, caste by birth,
child marriage, temple priestcraft) a corruption; his *Satyarth
Prakash* (1875) set out the creed. The Samaj championed widow
remarriage, intercaste and even inter-religious marriage, women's
education, and **shuddhi** — reconversion — and built the D.A.V.
College, Lahore (1886) into a network of schools.

[important title="Two Arya Samajs"]
After Dayananda's death (by poisoning in 1883) the Samaj split
(1893): the **D.A.V. wing** leaned to English education and
college-building; the **Gurukul wing** (Gurukul Kangri, 1902)
wanted a fully Vedic curriculum. Both kept the Samaj the most
organisationally vigorous reform body in north India, feeding
both nationalism and the cow-protection movement.
[/important]

## 6. Ramakrishna and Vivekananda

**Ramakrishna Paramhansa** (1836-1886), priest of the Kali temple
at Dakshineshwar, taught by parable and trance that every religion
is a true road to the same God. His disciple **Narendranath Datta**
— **Swami Vivekananda** (1863-1902) — carried the message
abroad: his Chicago address at the **Parliament of Religions,
September 1893** made the West hear Vedanta from an Indian voice.
In 1897 he founded the **Ramakrishna Mission** — motto: "For
one's own salvation and for the good of the world" — joining
monastic spirituality with famine relief, schools, and hospitals,
and building Belur Math near Calcutta.

## 7. The Aligarh Movement

**Sir Syed Ahmad Khan** (1817-1898) drew the bitter lesson of 1857
in his *Asbab-e-Baghawat-e-Hind* (1859): Muslims needed modern
education and goodwill with the British. He founded the
**Scientific Society** (1864) to translate works of science, the
journal *Tahzib-ul-Akhlaq* (1870), and the **Muhammadan
Anglo-Oriental College at Aligarh (1875)** — which became the
Aligarh Muslim University in 1920. The **Aligarh Movement** built
a professional Muslim intelligentsia; after the Congress was born
(1885), Sir Syed counselled Muslims to stand apart, a counsel
critics trace to the separate-electorate politics that followed.

