## 8. The Wider Reform Map

@diagram tree id="reform-streams" layout="tree" aria="The main streams of nineteenth-century reform and their organisations"
  Reform movements -> "Hindu: Brahmo Samaj (1828)"
  Reform movements -> "Hindu: Prarthana Samaj (1867)"
  Reform movements -> "Hindu: Arya Samaj (1875)"
  Reform movements -> "Hindu: Ramakrishna Mission (1897)"
  Reform movements -> "Muslim: Aligarh Movement (1875)"
  Reform movements -> "Muslim: Deoband (1866)"
  Reform movements -> "Sikh: Singh Sabha (1873)"
  Reform movements -> "Parsi: Rahnumai Mazdayasnan (1851)"
  Reform movements -> "Anti-caste: Satyashodhak Samaj (1873)"
@/diagram

| Organisation | Founder and year | Focus |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Brahmo Samaj | Rammohan Roy, 1828 | Monotheism, anti-sati, women's rights |
| Prarthana Samaj | Atmaram Pandurang, 1867 (Ranade, Bhandarkar) | Devotional reform, widow remarriage |
| Rahnumai Mazdayasnan Sabha | Naoroji and others, 1851 | Parsi social reform |
| Deoband Darul Uloom | Nanautavi and Gangohi, 1866 | Islamic scholarship and revival |
| Arya Samaj | Dayananda Saraswati, 1875 | Back to the Vedas; shuddhi |
| Singh Sabha | Amritsar, 1873 | Sikh revival and education |
| Satyashodhak Samaj | Jyotirao Phule, 1873 | Anti-caste equality; Satyashodhak weddings |
| Ramakrishna Mission | Vivekananda, 1897 | Vedanta plus service |
| Theosophical Society | Olcott and Blavatsky, 1875; Adyar 1882; Annie Besant from 1893 | Universal brotherhood, Indian pride |

The deepest social challenge came from **Jyotirao and Savitribai
Phule**: their school for girls at Pune (1848) was among the first
run by Indians, and his *Gulamgiri* (1873) attacked Brahmanical
domination itself. In Kerala **Sri Narayana Guru** consecrated
his own Shiva temple (1888) and taught "one caste, one religion,
one God for man" (his SNDP Yogam followed in 1903). The
Theosophists under **Annie Besant** revived pride in Indian
philosophy and built the Central Hindu College (1898) that became
part of Banaras Hindu University.

[note]
The movement's limits are as historical as its triumphs: leaders
were overwhelmingly urban, male, and upper caste; practice lagged
behind preaching; and Hindu, Muslim, Sikh, and Parsi reform
sometimes sharpened communal self-consciousness even as it fought
superstition. Yet the organisations, journals, and petitions of
the reform age were the training ground of the national movement
itself.
[/note]

